{"schema_version":"1.9.2","id":"GHSA-gmj8-hvj3-49mw","published":"2026-11-10T21:25:18Z","modified":"2026-11-10T21:45:03.954839319Z","aliases":["CVE-2026-126067"],"summary":"Action Pack: Missing SameSite attribute on session cookies from Action Pack session stores","details":"### Impact\nAction Pack session stores other than `ActionDispatch::Session::CookieStore` (`CacheStore`, `MemCacheStore`, and third-party stores built on `AbstractStore`, `AbstractSecureStore` or the session mixins) write the session cookie without a `SameSite` attribute, regardless of `config.action_dispatch.cookies_same_site_protection`, which defaults to `:lax`. Only `CookieStore` applies the configured policy. In browsers that do not treat a cookie without the attribute as `SameSite=Lax`, a cross-site request could carry the session cookie, possibly leading to cross-site request forgery against actions that do not verify an authenticity token, such as those of API-only applications.\n\nApplications are affected when they use a session store other than `CookieStore` and do not pass `same_site:` to `config.session_store`.\n\n### Details\nIn the fixed releases, every session store sets the session cookie's `SameSite` attribute from `cookies_same_site_protection` when its options have no `:same_site` key. An explicit `same_site:` option, including `same_site: nil`, and a `:same_site` value in a store's `DEFAULT_OPTIONS` still take precedence.\n\n### Workarounds\nPass `same_site:` when configuring the session store, for example `config.session_store :cache_store, same_site: :lax`.\n\n### Releases\nThe fixed releases are available at the normal locations.\n\nPatches for those who cannot upgrade at once:\n- 8.1: https://github.com/rails/rails/commit/2d9f502df941e31ce9bad0e846564f49396bed46.patch\n- 8.0: https://github.com/rails/rails/commit/f2392996be723708271a8d30bfcf0edc221e53ef.patch\n\n### Credit\nThis issue was responsibly reported by HackerOne researcher [tjvc](https://hackerone.com/tjvc).","a